5:["$","article",null,{"className":"container-site py-14 md:py-20","children":[["$","$Le",null,{"href":"/blog","className":"group inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:text-foreground","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-left h-4 w-4 transition-transform duration-300 group-hover:-translate-x-0.5 motion-reduce:transform-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","path","1l729n",{"d":"m12 19-7-7 7-7"}],["$","path","x3x0zl",{"d":"M19 12H5"}],"$undefined"]}],"All writing"]}],["$","header",null,{"className":"mt-8 max-w-2xl","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 font-mono text-[12px] tracking-wide text-muted-foreground/70","children":[["$","time",null,{"dateTime":"2026-10-18","children":"October 18, 2026"}],["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"h-px w-6 bg-border"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5","children":[["$","svg",null,{"ref":"$undefined","xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-clock h-3 w-3","aria-hidden":"true","children":[["$","path","mmk7yg",{"d":"M12 6v6l4 2"}],["$","circle","1mglay",{"cx":"12","cy":"12","r":"10"}],"$undefined"]}],6," min read"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl","children":"Notes from self-hosting my own infrastructure"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":"What running Coolify, Docker, PostgreSQL and DNS by hand teaches that managed platforms never will — a field report from my own deployment practice."}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex flex-wrap gap-2","aria-label":"Tags","children":[["$","li","DevOps",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"DevOps"}],["$","li","Self-hosting",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Self-hosting"}],["$","li","Linux",{"className":"label-mono rounded-md border border-border bg-card px-2 py-1 text-muted-foreground","children":"Linux"}]]}]]}],["$","$Lf",null,{"className":"mt-12","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-2xl space-y-5","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"There is a moment every self-hoster knows. You type the domain into the browser, the page fails to load, and you realise the cause could live in any one of five places: the registrar, the DNS resolver, the proxy, the container, or the application itself. Nothing tells you which. That moment — more than any feature — is why I self-host."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"I run my own deployment environment on an Ubuntu host with Coolify: applications deploy from GitHub repositories into containers, a proxy layer terminates TLS and routes my configured domains, and PostgreSQL and Redis back the services that need them. It is not a production platform serving customers under an SLA. It is a personal workbench, and it has quietly reorganised how I think about software."}],["$","h2","2",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"Managed platforms teach you the abstraction"}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"I do not think managed platforms are bad — they are often the right engineering decision. But they teach you their interface, not the system underneath. Push to deploy, and a hundred deliberate decisions happen on your behalf: which ports open, how TLS is renewed, where logs land, what happens when a container dies at 3am. You learn the button. You do not learn the machine."}],"$L10","$L11","$L12","$L13","$L14","$L15","$L16","$L17","$L18","$L19","$L1a"]}]}],"$L1b"]}]
10:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Operating the stack myself forced the questions into the open. When a deployment fails behind a proxy, the 502 you see is a symptom, never the cause. The cause is one layer deeper — in the container's exit logs, or the proxy's error log, or the application's own stdout. Nobody surfaces this for you. You develop a reading habit: container logs, then proxy logs, then application logs, in that order, every time."}]
11:["$","h2","5",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"DNS is where patience is manufactured"}]
12:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"My hardest-won lesson had nothing to do with containers. DNS records propagate asynchronously, resolvers cache aggressively, and 'it works for me' is a meaningless sentence in a world where different resolvers hold different opinions about your domain. Early on, I reconfigured a perfectly correct setup out of impatience, breaking something that was quietly on its way to working."}]
13:["$","p","7",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Now I follow a fixed diagnostic order — registrar first, then resolver caches, then direct DNS queries, then the proxy — and I wait before changing anything. Boring discipline beats clever debugging. The DNS zone that resolves eventually and the one you broke in a hurry look identical from the outside."}]
14:["$","blockquote","8",{"className":"rounded-lg border-l-2 border-accent-blue bg-muted/30 px-6 py-5","children":["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[15px] italic leading-relaxed text-foreground/90","children":"Logs before restarts. One variable at a time. Boring is a feature you install in yourself."}]}]
15:["$","h2","9",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"The habits transfer everywhere"}]
16:["$","p","10",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"What surprised me most is how portable the discipline turned out to be. The habit of reading logs in sequence is the same skill as reading a stack trace properly. The instinct to change one variable at a time is the same instinct that makes debugging scientific instead of superstitious. The reflex to verify each layer — locally, then through the firewall, then from outside — is just the scientific method wearing an operations uniform."}]
17:["$","ul","11",{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"Logs before restarts — a restart destroys the evidence you need."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"One variable at a time — two changes teach you nothing about either."]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"Verify layer by layer — every failing layer will announce itself if you ask in order."]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":"true","className":"mt-2.5 h-1 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-accent-blue/60"}],"Backups you haven't restored are superstition, not safety."]}]]}]
18:["$","h2","12",{"className":"mt-12 border-t border-border pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight first:mt-8","children":"Would I recommend it?"}]
19:["$","p","13",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"If you are a student of systems — of how the web actually delivers a request from a URL to a process and back — self-hosting is the most honest education available. You will lose evenings to DNS. You will learn what a reverse proxy actually does because you configured its routing yourself. And the first time a service you deployed stays up for a month, quietly serving requests through infrastructure you understand end-to-end, something clicks that no tutorial can manufacture."}]
1a:["$","p","14",{"className":"text-[15px] leading-[1.75] text-muted-foreground","children":"Start small: one host, one service, one domain. Add PostgreSQL when something needs it, Redis when something needs that. Let the infrastructure be pulled into existence by real projects rather than pushed into shape by enthusiasm. That is how mine grew, and it is the only way I would recommend growing one."}]
